<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Panchayat</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as panchayats</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A panchayat is a traditional village council in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a>, typically composed of elders. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha Empire</a> panchayats managed agrarian disputes and customary law. They worked alongside provincial officials responsible for justice, revenue and defence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/panchayat.jpg" alt="Panchayat" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Shijualex at Malayalam Wikipedia · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Muhamma_grama_panchayath.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The word means an assembly (ayat) of five (panch). Self-governing village bodies called sabhas appear in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rigveda">Rigveda</a>, and over time such bodies developed into panchayats with executive and judicial powers: they distributed land, collected taxes on produce and paid the government's share for the village, under a larger council that could intervene. A temple wall at Uthiramerur in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a> records how a medieval South Indian village council worked. Thirty members were chosen by lot from men of good character who met conditions of landholding and scriptural learning, and they served on committees for irrigation works, gardens and other matters.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Casteism and the feudal system of governance under Mughal rule gradually eroded village self-government, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamindar">zamindars</a>, a new class of feudal chiefs and revenue collectors, came between the ruler and the people. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>, granted the office of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diwan">Diwan</a> in 1765, replaced the village land record office with its own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/village-accountant">Patwari</a> and the village police with a magistrate acting through the Darogha. These measures, meant to collect land revenue, disempowered the village community. After 1857 the British gave panchayats powers to try minor offences and settle village disputes, and by 1925 eight provinces had passed panchayat acts.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a> saw the village panchayat as the foundation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s political system, a vision he called Gram Swaraj, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/b-r-ambedkar">B. R. Ambedkar</a> regarded the village as a source of oppression. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-india">Constitution of India</a> placed village panchayats among the Directive Principles of State Policy, as Article 40. A committee under Balwant Rai Mehta recommended in 1958 a three-tier structure of district, block and village councils, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jawaharlal-nehru">Jawaharlal Nehru</a> inaugurated the panchayati raj system at Nagaur, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a>, on 2 October 1959, Gandhi's birthday.</p><p>The Panchayati Raj Act, the 73rd Amendment of 1992, brought regular elections every five years and reserved seats for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scheduled-castes">Scheduled Castes</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scheduled-tribes">Scheduled Tribes</a> and women, who receive not less than one third. The elected gram panchayats of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> are distinct both from the traditional councils of elders and from the khap or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> panchayats of northern India.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Panchayati raj” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Districts were governed by appointed officers such as subedars responsible for revenue and defence, while village assemblies (<b>panchayats</b>) settled disputes according to customary law.</blockquote><a href="/empire/maratha-empire">Maratha Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Khalsa army, unpaid and politically radicalised, increasingly dictated policy through its elected regimental committees (<b>panchayats</b>).</blockquote><a href="/empire/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Bhawanrao Pant Pratinidhi, who reigned from 1909 to 1947, promoted the surya namaskar exercise and the arts, and in 1938, with the advice of Gandhi, introduced the Aundh Experiment, handing power to village <b>panchayats</b> under a constitution drafted with Maurice Frydman.</blockquote><a href="/state/aundh-state">Aundh State</a></li></ul></section>
